7. If the name is too short (less than 4 letters), the variable name does not get a value and stays null. when the program tries to print the name in uppercase, it fails because you can’t do that on something that is null. This causes a NullPointerException error.
To fix this, we can put the call to printNameUpperCase inside a try and catch block. If an error happens, instead of the program crashing, we can throw a different error like an IllegalArgumentException with a clear message that says the name must be longer. This makes the code easier to understand and helps people know what went wrong.

8.The loop goes from i = 0 to i <= arr.length but arrays start at 0 and go up to (arr.length - 1). So when i is equal to arr.length, the program tries to use an index that doesnt exist. This causes the ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.
To fix this we can keep the loop as it is but add a try and catch around it. This will catch the error when it happens and print a message instead of crashing the program.
But we can also just change the loop condition from  (i<= arr.length) to (i < arr.length)...
